# Code owners for meridian-launch-systems / azure-devsecops-demo
#
# GitHub requests a review from the owner of every path a pull request touches.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES TODAY: it ROUTES review requests. It does not REQUIRE them.
#
# This header used to say "paired with branch protection requiring review, this is
# the control that stops a workflow, a role grant or a compliance record changing
# without the person accountable for it seeing the diff" - and cited NIST SP 800-171
# Rev 2 3.4.3 and 3.1.5 for it. That pairing did not exist: main's ruleset sets
# required_approving_review_count to 0, so no approval has ever been required and
# every merge in this repository's history was self-approved. A control asserted in
# prose and enforced nowhere is the exact defect this repository exists to catch,
# and it sat in the file that describes the control.
#
# It is not an oversight - it is a DELIBERATE mode, now written down where it can be
# read and checked: .github/governance-mode.json declares `development`, in which
# agents are authorized to self-approve and merge on green because nothing here
# reaches a real user and the gauntlet is the safety gate.
#
# The paths below become genuinely review-gated on the move to `operational` mode,
# which is one setting: required_approving_review_count 0 -> 1. They are the paths
# that define what "safe" MEANS, so a change to them can weaken the gate that
# authorised it. verification/layer-01-audit.ps1 V1.5 fails whenever the declared
# mode and the enforced ruleset disagree, so that transition cannot be half-done.
#
# NIST SP 800-171 Rev 2 3.4.3 ("track, review, approve or disapprove, and log
# changes to organizational systems") and 3.1.5 (least privilege) are what the
# operational-mode gate evidences. In development mode they are evidenced by the
# gauntlet and the audit trail, not by human approval - state it that way in the
# compliance register rather than claiming a review gate that is switched off.
#
# Ordering matters: the LAST matching pattern wins, so the broad default comes
# first and the sensitive paths override it.

# Everything, unless a rule below is more specific.
*                                   @paulcfuqua

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The paths where an unreviewed change does the most damage.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# CI/CD. A workflow change can mint an OIDC token, push an image, or write to
# the default branch. This is the highest-value target in the repository.
/.github/workflows/                 @paulcfuqua
/.github/actions/                   @paulcfuqua
/.github/CODEOWNERS                 @paulcfuqua

# Infrastructure as code. Ingress, identity, role assignments and policy all
# live here; so does every "who can reach what" decision in the estate.
/infra/                             @paulcfuqua

# Identity and tenant-level objects. Entra users, groups, app registrations and
# Conditional Access, plus the three G3 teardown scripts that delete them.
/infra/entra/                       @paulcfuqua
/infra/purview/                     @paulcfuqua
/infra/policy/                      @paulcfuqua

# Bootstrap. OIDC federation, the budget, and the Fabric capacity - the scripts
# that run before any gate exists to catch them.
/scripts/bootstrap/                 @paulcfuqua

# The compliance register and the platform that renders it. A change here does
# not alter the estate, but it alters what the estate CLAIMS about itself, which
# is the one thing this repository exists to keep honest.
/compliance/                        @paulcfuqua

# The Verifier. Its audits are the authoritative test for whether a layer is
# done; weakening one is indistinguishable from passing it.
/verification/                      @paulcfuqua

# Security policy and the findings record.
/SECURITY.md                        @paulcfuqua
/CLAUDE.md                          @paulcfuqua
